---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StatusPill from '../../components/StatusPill.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Reusable Patterns"
  description="Create reusable patterns from selected vector artwork, imported raster tiles, or a procedural recipe, then place them independently on objects in Varve."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <main class="pattern-feature">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Reusable Patterns
      </nav>

      <header class="feature-hero">
        <StatusPill status="experimental" label="Source-build preview" />
        <h1>Make a repeat once. Place it where the design needs it.</h1>
        <p class="feature-intro">
          Build a reusable pattern from copied vector artwork, an image tile, or a procedural
          recipe. Apply that definition to selected objects, then adjust each fill’s tile scale,
          phase, and rotation in the inspector.
        </p>
        <p class="release-note">
          This pattern library is available in the current source build and is not included in a
          published Varve release yet.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="model-card site-card site-card--surface" aria-labelledby="model-title">
        <div class="model-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Four things, kept distinct</p>
          <h2 id="model-title">Source, repeat, definition, and placement have separate jobs.</h2>
          <p>
            A copied motif or tile is the source. Its definition stores cell and repeat data. An
            object’s fill points to that reusable definition and owns its own placement values.
            Editing those placement values does not resize or move the original artwork on the
            canvas.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pattern-flow" aria-label="Pattern structure: source becomes a reusable definition, which is placed as a fill on an object">
          <div class="flow-step"><span>01 · SOURCE</span><strong>Artwork or tile</strong><small>Copied vector, raster image, or recipe</small></div>
          <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <div class="flow-step flow-step--accent"><span>02 · DEFINITION</span><strong>Reusable pattern</strong><small>Cell bounds and repeat settings</small></div>
          <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <div class="flow-step"><span>03 · APPLICATION</span><strong>One object fill</strong><small>Independent scale, phase, and rotation</small></div>
        </div>
        <p class="diagram-note">Conceptual model — this diagram is not an application screenshot.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="capabilities" aria-labelledby="today-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">In the source-build preview</p>
        <h2 id="today-title">A practical start for reusable surface design.</h2>
        <div class="capability-grid">
          <article>
            <h3>Copy a vector selection</h3>
            <p>
              Create a definition from selected artwork in the Paint inspector. The selected source
              artwork remains on the canvas; the pattern keeps its own copied source data.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Bring in a raster tile</h3>
            <p>
              Import PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF, or AVIF. The image is embedded as a document asset, so the
              definition does not depend on its original file path.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Start with a recipe</h3>
            <p>
              New pattern creates a checkerboard recipe that is saved with the document, alongside
              its generated preview.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Apply and make unique</h3>
            <p>
              Apply a library entry to selected objects. A fill can be made unique for one selected
              object, with its own definition identity for later source changes.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Adjust the fill in place</h3>
            <p>
              Set tile width, tile height, horizontal and vertical phase, and rotation for the
              selected fill. The current repeat preview helps inspect the applied arrangement.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <h3>Keep assets with the file</h3>
            <p>
              Pattern definitions and imported tile bytes are part of document data. The normal
              document save path carries their source and dependencies together.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <aside class="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Current boundaries</p>
        <h2 id="limits-title">Source editing and some output paths are still being built.</h2>
        <p>
          The current UI creates, lists, applies, and makes definitions unique; definition settings
          can change the shared cell size and repeat arrangement. A library source can be replaced
          with an imported raster tile, which updates every linked use; make a fill unique first
          when only one use should change. The UI does not yet open a contextual session to edit the
          shared vector source. Raster edge repair, wraparound painting, shared page-origin
          alignment, and finite expansion are not available here. Export Tile saves one source
          cell; Export Supertile saves the full rectangular period for grid and conventional
          half-drop or brick repeats with mirror parity. Applied-fill SVG keeps supported
          linked grid patterns as reusable vector pattern definitions; unsupported cases use a
          raster fallback with a warning. PDF currently supports axis-aligned grids with positive
          gaps and reports unsupported transforms. PDF/X-1a omits transparent tiles or fills with
          non-opaque opacity.
        </p>
        <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/patterns')}>Read the pattern workflow and limits →</a>
      </aside>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .pattern-feature { max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: var(--type-interface-caption-size); margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .feature-hero { max-width: 760px; margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-hero h1 { color: var(--text-primary); font: 700 clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.25rem)/1.05 var(--font-editorial); letter-spacing: -.03em; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .feature-intro { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: var(--type-content-lead-size); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 720px; }
  .release-note { border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--border-accent); color: var(--text-secondary); margin-top: 1.25rem; padding-inline-start: 1rem; }
  .model-card, .limits { background: var(--surface-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-card); padding: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.5rem); }
  .eyebrow { color: var(--accent-primary); font-size: var(--type-interface-micro-size); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .model-copy { max-width: 700px; }
  .model-copy h2, .capabilities h2, .limits h2 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.25rem); line-height: 1.2; margin: .5rem 0 1rem; }
  .model-copy p:not(.eyebrow), .limits p:not(.eyebrow) { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; }
  .pattern-flow { align-items: stretch; display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .flow-step { background: var(--surface-muted); border: 1px solid var(--border-default); border-radius: var(--website-radius-surface); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .6rem; min-width: 0; padding: 1rem; }
  .flow-step--accent { background: var(--surface-accent-soft); border-color: var(--border-accent); }
  .flow-step span { color: var(--text-tertiary); font-size: var(--type-interface-micro-size); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em; }
  .flow-step strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .flow-step small { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.45; }
  .flow-arrow { align-self: center; color: var(--accent-primary); font-size: var(--type-display-section-size); }
  .diagram-note { color: var(--text-tertiary); font-size: var(--type-interface-caption-size); margin: 1rem 0 0; text-align: center; }
  .capabilities { padding: 4rem 0; }
  .capability-grid { display: grid; gap: 2rem 1.5rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .capability-grid article { border-top: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-top: 1rem; }
  .capability-grid h3 { color: var(--text-primary); margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .capability-grid p { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.65; margin: 0; }
  .limits a { color: var(--text-link); font-weight: 600; }
  @media (max-width: 720px) { .pattern-flow { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .flow-arrow { justify-self: center; transform: rotate(90deg); } .capability-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
